WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.128
Serving LLMs over APIs turns model inference into an operational service.

00:00:05.128 --> 00:00:19.809
The model becomes one component inside an architecture that includes clients, gateways, backends, inference servers, runtimes, model files, tokenizers, compute resources, queues, logs, monitoring, and storage.

00:00:19.809 --> 00:00:26.271
The service boundary is where reliability, security, privacy, and application behavior meet.

00:00:26.271 --> 00:00:40.391
Strong implementations combine clear architecture, suitable model and runtime choices, secure API design, controlled exposure, performance management, privacy-aware logging, and operational guardrails.

00:00:40.391 --> 00:00:53.175
They document working configurations, define authentication and authorization, limit request size and rate, validate structured output, monitor health, and handle failure gracefully.

00:00:53.175 --> 00:01:00.200
Privacy and data handling are central because prompts and outputs can contain sensitive information.

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:07.575
Logging, retention, analytics, prompt history, backups, and debug traces should be designed deliberately.

00:01:07.575 --> 00:01:16.637
Local or private serving can improve control, but only if the surrounding service does not create new data leaks or access paths.

00:01:16.637 --> 00:01:24.996
The practical goal is reliable, observable, least-privileged, privacy-aware inference that applications can use safely.

00:01:24.996 --> 00:01:28.087
A model that responds is only the beginning.

00:01:28.087 --> 00:01:40.660
A production-quality LLM API makes access explicit, behavior measurable, failures understandable, data handling intentional, and downstream use controlled enough for real systems.
